          %p
            At the moment, this is a “place holder” page. I (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew Stacey
            ) want to learn about the appearance of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            and shall record what I learn here. A preliminary outline is to find out about the following statements:
          %ol
            %li
              The category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
              with linear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+maps"} short maps
              is not
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+category"} monadic
              over Set. The “nearest” algebraic theory is that of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+convex+spaces"} totally convex spaces
            %li
              The category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebras"} Banach algebras
              is also not algebraic.
            %li
              The category of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebras
              is algebraic.
          %h3#banach_spaces Banach Spaces
          %p
            We consider the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
            with linear
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+maps"} short maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ban
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Ban}
            with:
          %ul
            %li
              Objects: Banach spaces over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %li
              Morphisms
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon E \to F
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|T\| \le 1
          %p
            We define a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ban
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \colon \operatorname{Ban} \to \operatorname{Set}
            sending a Banach space to its unit ball. Since linear short maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to F
            take the unit ball of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            into the unit ball of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            There is a functor in the opposite direction which assigns to a set the “free” Banach space on that set. That is, it assigns to a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the Banach space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(X)
            of all absolutely summable sequences indexed by elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is.
          #bspadj.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1
              is left adjoint to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              We need to define the adjunction natural transformations:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %msup
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_X \colon X \to B \ell^1(X)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mi E
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_E \colon \ell^1(B E) \to E
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              the sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\delta_{x y})
              which is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              elsewhere. The second is the summation map which assigns to an absolutely summable sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_e)
              indexed by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in B E
              its sum,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi e
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum a_e e
          %p
            This adjunction defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Set}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon \operatorname{Set} \to \operatorname{Set}
            sends a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the unit ball of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X)
            is a weighted (formal) sum of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum a_x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum |a_x| \le 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            to the delta sequences in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum a_s
            where each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum s_x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\sum a_s) = \sum b_x
            where
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_x = \sum_s s_x
          .query
            %p AS: I think! I need to check exactly how the product works in this example but I’m just getting the basic sketch down first.
          %p
            The key question is whether or not
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ban
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Ban}
            is (equivalent to) the category of algebras for this monad. That is, is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ban
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \colon \operatorname{Ban} \to \operatorname{Set}
            = succeed "?" do
              %em tripleable
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck%27s+tripleability+theorem"} Beck's tripleability theorem
            gives three conditions for a functor to be tripleable. We already have one (the adjunction), let us show that the second also holds.
          #bsprefiso.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ban
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \colon \operatorname{Ban} \to \operatorname{Set}
              reflects isomorphisms.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon E \to F
              be a linear short map which induces an isomorphism on the unit balls of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              to that of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|T(x)\| = \|x\|
              (so that its inverse is a short map as well). This is simple to show: if we had some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in E
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|x\| = 1
              but
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|T(x)\| \lt 1
              (if it fails, it must fail that way as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is short) then there would be some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \gt 1
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|T(\lambda x)\| \le 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(T) \colon B E \to B F
              is surjective, there is some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in B E
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(y) = T(\lambda x)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \gt 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∉
                    %mi B
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x \notin B E
              so
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x \ne y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructively
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              that any number that is neither greater nor smaller than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              must equal
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          .query
            %p AS: To be continued …
          %h4#references References:
          %p
            The above is essentially
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"} my
            “notes” on reading the following (and whatever necessary to understand the following):
